# Narzedzia do weryfikacji WebUI (jsdom/playwright)
node_modules/

# package-lock.json is NOT ignored, and the comment above used to sit on both
# lines, which is how it ended up here. An ignored lockfile is a lockfile nobody
# reads: it was 117 packages with no prettier and no playwright in it, while
# package.json declared four devDependencies. The two disagreed and CI never
# noticed, because CI did not read the lockfile either -- it installed
# playwright with --no-save. So the file was decoration.
#
# It matters twice over now. npm ci deletes node_modules and reinstalls from this
# file, so it is the only thing that makes the WebUI gates reproducible, and it
# is what pins prettier to the exact version that formats html`...` the way the
# checked-in bundle is formatted.

# Signing keys.

# debug.keystore IS committed on purpose: CI and local builds must share one
# debug signature or the app cannot be updated in place across the two. It is a
# published, worthless key and must never sign a shipped build.

# A release key is not worthless, so it never gets committed. Named explicitly
# rather than as a blanket *.keystore/*.jks, because that pattern would also
# exclude debug.keystore and silently break the shared debug signature above.
release.keystore
*.release.keystore
*.release.jks
*.p12
*.pfx
local-signing.properties
